KNOWNBUG: [expr.const] / [temp.inst] -- a call to a constexpr static member
function *template* with explicit template arguments (TC::ok<int>()) must be a
constant expression.  CBMC folds a plain constexpr function call but resolves a
member-function-template call to the generic (un-instantiated) template, whose
body still references the template parameter; the constexpr mini-interpreter
(try_evaluate_constexpr) then cannot fold it, so it is rejected with "expected
constant expression, but got 'ok()'".  This is the remaining blocker for
std::get<i> on a std::tuple: libstdc++'s tuple constructors are constrained by
such constexpr static member function templates
(_TupleConstraints::__is_explicitly_constructible<...>() etc.) in their enable_if
conditions, so the tuple cannot be constructed and std::get is silently dropped.
Flip to CORE once constexpr member-function-template calls are instantiated and
evaluated as constant expressions.
